ace.\n\n![](images/31/317fe507f591931ea371db43b78a83b1741c427ef89dd90571f0a0030ed70c20.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n![](images/87/878b11e2e3f3f3aaa6133482d483f1aa22ff420ade2a3d9533692f04c47348d1.svg)\nthus, ![](images/e2/e2c67900a3ba263aad1f200c892be4d755ab441e6b96ad6317e9364121f597b4.svg) is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ![](images/41/413cdb74711dd235ff727690ee5fb8713e6f50cbb49d131121a40b18b0943f53.svg) solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ![](images/ed/ed99403d5ac94993d00ca1f9c99def2e8d3bcbddba68d8aa1c1a8651fd140e2a.svg) as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3a/3a6d8a97c6d07c78562c73dbd1abef1b1a3c0d9f38cd14f4f9874df091492b9e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166543 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166543 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-6x(t)-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T,$ respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186166543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c7567cf-f861-5088-b43e-ff94fcff0c91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166543 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/9b/9b19dee8e197c29ab1802c7ea500f07185f24885702027a9726de39a91eacd9e.svg)\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/15/150fe59c158191129d89c43a1bb569e5c88b686c4365a6b06e661343f85b5e7d.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -\\dfrac12x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/f6/f6679165a59f48eb2944f4f8337ba990f2e82619e5e2a03308e0b4f663f9137c.svg)\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/e1/e1dcfabda7582534138b11b45a14cd8a8a9923c02d743ff99af08e487bcda254.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/3b/3ba48e81a2f6239f7cf0389017d823b87d558ded003eb6470ff6dc9078808b87.svg)\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point. \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/27/2710e11309547500aa3e9ba89f31281cbbc28244509046ed25a6c151c10c23ed.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166544 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166544 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/27/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "986f9190-9e73-5e08-b08d-ec779933b0cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166544 #edb/ref 1074 "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](images/0c/0c4b7d443c58e6720389306e73eb4e5e1934cfb7359221f36fb43ceb3e91e501.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/32/3244f8128366a3f7f4ae3e41cd9323b1712f9587943f9fb2de54ee0c458feedf.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/c3/c3ac6997c0e8580f8d91b88578a02043ca495e99e3ea67bdf91b3eab9eb43d98.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/77/77200c9fefd57e0b82b796072e07411f5fe10c91185d7d8f665f7ef6c3f54360.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/0c/0ce3220f2cd013daba6b1f44f88d2834021b44ba877980f5ec47df7bc52396ce.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166916 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketchi