92186168000 #edb/ref 1064 "Sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-4$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ![](images/38/38145b0a5b121bacb02c6099d44a67e4dc69f8bd56886520705f72bf8914c1be.svg) and ![](images/a7/a703180de3d09425c5851fb61ba1252f9678bae0d0d46f09841c558eddce2f53.svg) respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0f/0f26128d359ffa6aa2f5e970532cb45d17172035b8265232d79fb0f885727c9d.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/05/05929fabda1451bdfef2600375b1c2958170cad989a50dadd35ad4039fc5d9af.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny =-\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/08/0838cdd1e0a3d3603c24565276255fdb6d1eb2adf410f085058e954c5b153338.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/14/1459d79913dec89577405a506b8d4adbbf4cb05abb51948eedae76d8f71544b8.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/a3/a3350d2791e5dbb9662affd4dd64d36edae7a32198e0a3da5c6567a211ce3395.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/04/04d96c354e7d03cf0b88d0ee29bc7097b552b6959cf76c88012af8758794e0a9.svg)\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd53399b3d0c5ec40adcf91aaae4aca5ce55a9a7b574e9223fde615afc3b769f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf0c26af74326dd108d42eec1493b0ddf64cfdf0744c91b55e732b80b7d0835c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168000 #edb/ref 1065 14454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168000 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "031f896a-f5ca-51ae-a8e9-aa3cbdeb5224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168000 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example: One Positive and One Negative Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168007 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ![](images/d7/d71caa68afca9754733d4e2a297aa5797a0557d5f54e31f27b4a594179502739.svg) and ![](images/36/36065bd1dab3ced89501cf11b7891095c0e7b42e065a3bbdfa4bc5bd8a365368.svg) respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/95/95bb67ded561bb289e4fd81ddddcec6eec2685201a8e2e0fca42bbcecbccf3d2.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/5b/5bca9f7fd018a8177f885b099b73a471a70568e799564c94061d78ce23b2c9a6.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/1c/1c0def9e97c023fd4e71c146dae04d93cc0f6fed126a5c650f042f0500fda7d4.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/00/00bf863ed71e61386b9f755abfe7152e36474d68404204e0f1c0633828d3735c.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/32/3246939f411072280c878c2b055f329d69ff48568a9a8ad8301c0a0b04f58ddd.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c3/c3030613b03a1afabd964d9494bf2359bd6ce5f021c731feda9607a81aaf5df6.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1\n$$\n\nthus, $\\lambda_1 = 5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168007 #edb/ref 1065 14469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168007 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "399c5598-6334-5eba-a0dc-06d8a62a0dfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168007 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example: Two Positive Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168021 #edb/ref 1064 "In this topic, we'll study *phase portraits* of linear systems\n![](images/6f/6f6a92e2b8fb89f833268a5526876cb3af5215db0193a2aaaad8aac5790752ea.svg)\nin the case where the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two *real, distinct* eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c\\,e^{\\lambda t}\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\ngives us a solution that represents a line through the origin parallel to $\\mathbf{v}.$ So, when $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ are real and distinct, we always have two special straight-line solutions:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1 e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf{v}_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2\n$$\n\nAlong the line parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$:\n\n- If $\\lambda<0,$ then $e^{\\lambda t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ so trajectories move *toward* the origin (*stable line*).\n\n- If $\\lambda>0,$ then $e^{\\lambda t}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ so trajectories move *away* from the origin (*unstable line*).\n\nUsually, to sketch the phase portrait, we proceed as follows:\n\n1\\. Draw the two eigenvector lines through the origin (one line parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and another parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_2$).\n\n2\\. Add arrows on each eigenvector line:\n\n  - toward the origin if its eigenvalue is negative, or\n\n  - away from the origin if its eigenvalue is positive.\n\n3\\. Add a few curved trajectories that are generally \"pulled\" toward the stable direction(s) and \"pushed\" along unstable direction(s).\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168021 #edb/ref 1065 14566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168021 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc9385f4-31a9-5843-ac73-7335f2530b76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168021 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168022 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a linear system\n![](images/86/869f75b82137ebde181b22be0d6734196363444e9c53cb9e77deafc975931dd9.svg)\nin the case where the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two *real, distinct* eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nRecall that if $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2 \\neq 0,$ the system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$\nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst, we describe the straight-line solutions:\n\n- Along the line parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_1,$ solutions have the form $\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf{v}_1$ where $c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ it's a *stable* line (direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ it's an *unstable* line (direction).\n\n- Along the line parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ solutions have the form $\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2$ where $c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ it's a *stable* line (direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ it's an *unstable* line (direction).\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's apply this classification to concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168022 #edb/ref 1065 14567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168022 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "93564ac3-0171-5011-adda-f2d7ed859149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168022 #edb/ref 1067 "Classification of Equilibria" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305154 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305154 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305154 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00555be1-a348-5be5-af69-456ed748a4ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305163 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305163 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305163 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01495a53-7dfd-544b-a887-b616e881178d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305175 #edb/ref 1080 "{v}_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02935a6f-a709-5867-9ab8-c7df7f31cd7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305176 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02997696-3425-569b-b25e-4732dcfe7d0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305230 #edb/ref 1080 "x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305230 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305230 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "085ac6ca-782e-5863-a35b-7bb8b2fbc81c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305250 #edb/ref 1080 "y=x" #edb/ref 17592186045